lence  against women tackling violence against  women and girls is their number one  priority what's holding them back why  does the prime minister for example  today seem so downcast when it comes to  talking about progress and getting  prosecution rates up and achieving  targets that they themselves set earlier  this summer  it feels like there's you know like  always the government is really keen on  um coming up with policies and  statements that focus on the short term  and and because violence against women  is something that you know create it  needs a whole system to work together  and radical change to happen  for us to come you know anywhere close  to reducing the levels of abuse that  women face  they there is a lack of lack of  recognition of that so for example you  know we know that um the we know that  there's almost one woman every week  reports a police officer for sexual  assault or domestic abuse it's not new  we've heard from police officers many  times female police officers that say  they don't feel confident when they go  to uh have complaints against their male  uh counterparts that those in complaints  are going to be investigated more than  600 police officers have had complaints  of you know gender-based violence  against them  and in the last 10 years so we we none  of this is new what we need is radical  change in the way police are vetted  they're trained they get less than half  a day's training on trauma and  psychology at the moment  and like more robust processes when you  have complaints coming up against  officers that those are investigated  whether they're from the inside or  outside and more importantly you know  the police's databases don't match at  the moment they don't link up so if you  have a  an allegation a charge in one police  station there's a very good chance if  you are working another police station  or you are a perpetrator who's been  brought in into the station at another  place that will not get caught up and 